@node	0.9, 0.10, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Modules as values

@noindent
Section 0.3 introduced modules as a namespace, reached with a dot. A module
can also satisfy a stated contract, be built generically from another
module, and even be passed around as an ordinary value: three ideas this section introduces in turn, because chapter 2's typed-arithmetic appendix and
chapter 5's register-machine simulator both lean on them.

@subsubheading A signature is a contract

@code{module type} names a signature: a list of the types and values a
module must provide, with no implementation attached. Any module ascribed
against it must provide at least that much, at least as general as stated:

@example
# module type NUMBER = sig
    type t
    val zero : t
    val add : t -> t -> t
    val to_string : t -> string
  end;;
module type NUMBER =
  sig
    type t
    val zero : t
    val add : t -> t -> t
    val to_string : t -> string
  end
@end example

@noindent
@code{NUMBER} says nothing about what @code{t} is, only that
@emph{some} type @code{t} exists, with a zero, an addition, and a way to
render it. Two unrelated representations can both satisfy it:

@example
# module Int_number : NUMBER with type t = int = struct
    type t = int
    let zero = 0
    let add = ( + )
    let to_string = string_of_int
  end;;
module Int_number :
  sig
    type t = int
    val zero : t
    val add : t -> t -> t
    val to_string : t -> string
  end
@end example

@noindent
@code{NUMBER with type t = int} ascribes the module against @code{NUMBER}
while also stating that @code{t} is @code{int}, not merely some
unnamed type satisfying the signature. Without the @code{with type t = int}
clause, @code{Int_number.t} would be @newterm{abstract} outside this module: provably equal to @code{int} inside the @code{struct}, but opaque
to any code that only sees the ascribed signature. This edition adds the
clause here specifically so that later code can call, for instance,
@code{Int_number.add} directly on ordinary @code{int}s; leaving @code{t}
abstract is the right choice exactly when the point is to hide the
representation, which section 2.4's typed-arithmetic appendix does
deliberately.

@subsubheading A functor: a module parameterized by another module

A @newterm{functor} is a function from modules to modules, written like an ordinary function definition but over module-level arguments instead of value-level ones:

@example
# module Summer (M : NUMBER) = struct
    let sum xs = List.fold_left M.add M.zero xs
  end;;
module Summer : (M : NUMBER) -> sig val sum : M.t list -> M.t end
# module Float_number : NUMBER with type t = float = struct
    type t = float
    let zero = 0.0
    let add = ( +. )
    let to_string = Printf.sprintf "%g"
  end;;
module Float_number :
  sig
    type t = float
    val zero : t
    val add : t -> t -> t
    val to_string : t -> string
  end
# module Int_summer = Summer (Int_number);;
module Int_summer : sig val sum : Int_number.t list -> Int_number.t end
# module Float_summer = Summer (Float_number);;
module Float_summer :
  sig val sum : Float_number.t list -> Float_number.t end
# Int_summer.sum [ 1; 2; 3 ];;
- : Int_number.t = 6
# Float_summer.sum [ 1.5; 2.5 ];;
- : Float_number.t = 4.
@end example

@noindent
@code{Summer} is written exactly once and works over @emph{any} module
satisfying @code{NUMBER}; @code{Int_summer} and @code{Float_summer} are the
same code, instantiated twice. This is functors' whole purpose: one
algorithm, many representations, checked once against the abstract
signature rather than once per concrete instantiation. The raw
toplevel above reports @code{Int_summer.sum}'s result at type
@code{Int_number.t}, not bare @code{int}; @code{Int_number.t = int} is known here, but the type checker still prefers to print the more specific
name it has in scope. This edition's own @file{ch0/lib/sec_0_9.mli} seals
@code{Int_summer} behind a stated signature, @code{sig val sum : int list
-> int end}, so a reader of that library sees plain @code{int}
throughout, never @code{Int_number.t}: a small, concrete demonstration of
what ascribing a module against an interface actually buys.

@subsubheading A module packed as a value

@code{(module @var{M} : @var{S})} packs a module satisfying signature
@code{S} into an ordinary value of type @code{(module @var{S})}, which can
be stored in a record field, put in a list, or passed to a function like any other value: the same idea that closures gave functions in section 0.7, now at the module level:

@example
# type number_package = (module NUMBER);;
type number_package = (module NUMBER)
# let int_package : number_package = (module Int_number);;
val int_package : number_package = <module>
# let float_package : number_package = (module Float_number);;
val float_package : number_package = <module>
@end example

@noindent
Once packed, a @code{number_package} value has forgotten which concrete
module it came from: @code{int_package} and @code{float_package} are both
just @code{number_package}, and nothing about their type alone reveals
whether either hides @code{int} or @code{float}. Reading the package back
out uses the matching pattern @code{(module @var{M} : @var{S})}, which
un-hides its contents only inside the scope where it is unpacked:

@example
# let describe_zero (module M : NUMBER) = M.to_string M.zero;;
val describe_zero : (module NUMBER) -> string = <fun>
# describe_zero int_package;;
- : string = "0"
# describe_zero float_package;;
- : string = "0"
@end example

@noindent
@code{describe_zero} works on either package with no knowledge of which one
it is handed, because its result, a @code{string}, does not depend on the
hidden @code{M.t}. A function whose result type genuinely needs to be tied
to which module was supplied (@code{sum_with} below folds a list of @code{M.t} and renders the total) must instead take its module as a
@emph{literal} module expression at each call site, not as a value read out
of a variable, since only a literal @code{(module @dots{})} at the call site
lets the type checker resolve @code{M.t} for that one call:

@example
# let sum_with (module M : NUMBER) xs =
  M.to_string (List.fold_left M.add M.zero xs);;
val sum_with : (module M : NUMBER) -> M.t list -> string = <fun>
# sum_with (module Int_number) [ 1; 2; 3 ];;
- : string = "6"
# sum_with (module Float_number) [ 1.5; 2.5 ];;
- : string = "4"
@end example

@noindent
@code{sum_with}'s inferred type, @code{(module M : NUMBER) -> M.t list ->
string}, is a @newterm{dependent function type}: its second argument's type
depends on which module the first argument names. This is a genuine, if
narrow, OCaml feature; it is also the reason the packed values
@code{int_package} and @code{float_package} above could only be consumed by
a function like @code{describe_zero} whose result stays independent of the
hidden type, never by one like @code{sum_with} that needs its second
argument's element type tied to the module's own @code{M.t}. Chapter 2's
typed-arithmetic appendix and chapter 5's simulator both choose, case by
case, between a functor (one static representation per instantiation) and a
first-class package (one representation chosen at runtime), exactly along
this line.
